WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.120
Jordan: So obviously, the canon is made up
of those books that have had the most influence

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:05.990
and so they're the most fundamental.

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:10.530
Whether you agree with them or not is beside
the point, you need to know them because they're

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:11.880
at the bottom of your culture.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.780
And that's you.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:22.070
Christina: Jordan Peterson.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:23.070
Welcome to AEI.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.070
Jordan: Thank you.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:25.070
Nice to see you.

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:27.779
Christina: It's just so nice to find a fellow
traveler.

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:31.630
My God, I started out years ago with Camille
Paglia.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:36.460
We've been fighting this battle, but we have
stronger and stronger allies and we've got

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:37.460
you.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:38.550
And it's amazing.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:43.390
And when I hear you talk about topics that
have concerned me for years, I mean, the politicization

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:47.760
of the curriculum, the madness that's taken
over feminism.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:48.760
And...

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:50.870
Jordan: Think you're making any progress?

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:54.390
Christina: I don't know.

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:59.530
I get worried because 20 years ago or more
when Camille and I fought the first culture

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:07.980
war we thought we won and we did in the, you
know, court of public opinion, but they assumed

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.210
the assistant professorships.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:11.580
So they've been the teachers.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.210
They're in the schools of education and...

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:16.290
Jordan: Yeah, they're certainly in the schools
of education, that's for sure.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:22.310
Christina: And so sometimes I feel like just
common sense, open discussion debate, it's

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:24.760
all imperiled.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:28.930
And when you say so sometimes people think
you're a crank or you're overstating it.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:32.190
And I have a lot of self-doubt so I think,
"Well, maybe I am."

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:34.000
Jordan: Well, it's not easy to tell.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.140
Christina: Well, then James Damore happens,
you know, and he gets fired for saying...

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:39.200
Jordan: Saying what was true.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.200
Christina: ...what was true.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.280
Jordan: And what has been increasingly documented
as true since he got fired.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:45.280
Right?

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:46.280
By huge scale studies.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:51.310
Christina: And what everybody says and knows,
but now it's apparently dangerous to state

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:54.490
the obvious given the current orthodoxies.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:02:00.411
So to me, it's just been wonderful that you
have found a way around it.

00:02:00.411 --> 00:02:05.450
It's not that you were able to defeat what
was going on at the university because you

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:11.420
had maybe accidentally just this insane encounter
when you were told how you had to speak and

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:17.950
you protested, but now you've taken another
path and you're reaching millions of people

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:21.150
through your videos and then, you know, the
lectures and videos of lectures.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:27.030
And I'm just wondering if that's not the future
of education is a kind of alternative learning

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.030
system.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:33.600
Jordan: Well, I think it's the present of
education to some degree, I mean because people

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.670
you can find out about anything you want on
YouTube.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:44.140
Now there's part of it that's a bit anarchic,
let's say because...

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:45.630
Christina: There are false prophets.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:46.630
Jordan: Well, that's it.

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:47.630
That's it.

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:48.630
That's the thing.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:53.840
It's not that easy to distinguish between
valid and invalid information.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:59.130
I mean, when we had more reliable gatekeepers
in place.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:05.400
Let's say back in the heyday of journalism
when Time magazine was still, you know, three-eighths

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.170
of an inch thick and 90% print.

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:13.350
Then there was some consensus on what constituted
reliable and valid information.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:19.940
And YouTube you can learn anything about anything
and maybe that's not exactly optimal, but

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:24.480
I think it's reasonable to have faith in people's
ability to sift through the chaff and get

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:25.610
to the wheat.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:27.310
And so maybe it's a good thing overall.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:32.620
Christina: I mean, I do think, you know, in
the future people will look back and say,

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:33.620
"Well, that was anarchic.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:37.610
And you know, it's amazing anybody got any,
like, education."

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.320
But I think people are discerning.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:42.170
I don't think it's an accident that you sort
of rose to the top.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:47.950
I mean, I accidentally happened upon one of
your videos and it's just amazing like, "Wow,

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:49.480
he's making so much sense."

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:54.960
And at the time you weren't addressing culture
war issues and feminism but maybe postmodernism

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.310
and some related issues.

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:01.819
And I found you on YouTube and then started
following you.

00:04:01.819 --> 00:04:04.970
And I find, even if I look at the comments,
and I've looked at the comments on your videos,

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:08.099
I have a lot of very smart followers.

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:10.760
The comments are our civil.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:15.650
I think, maybe you have better, but I think
I have some of the most intelligent comments

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:16.650
on YouTube.

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:17.650
Jordan: Yeah.

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:21.180
Well, YouTube can be quite the hell of comments.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:22.280
Christina: But I don't get them.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:23.280
Jordan: No.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.960
And most of the comments on my YouTube videos
are civil and reasonable

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:29.840
Christina: Because people have to listen and
you know, it takes patience and attention.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:32.540
So I think the trolls, you know, they lose
interest.

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:33.540
The attention span.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:34.620
Jordan: Yeah, could be.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:41.630
Well and you know, on this tour that I've
been doing, one of the things that makes me

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:48.720
optimistic is the fact that I'm presenting,
I would say difficult material and in a relatively

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.010
difficult way.

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:53.980
Certainly, I would say it's intellectually
challenging as I can manage and it's also

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:59.270
emotionally challenging and there's an immense
public demand for it and there's an immense

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:05.370
public hunger for long-form discussions of
the type that no one thought a television

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:07.830
audience so to speak was capable of engaging
in.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:13.740
So I have a sneaking suspicion that people
are a lot smarter than our previous communication

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:19.101
technologies revealed and you know, people
are listening to podcasts like mad [SP] too

00:05:19.101 --> 00:05:24.630
and lengthy ones and audiobook sales are spiking
because it looks like more people can listen

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:26.160
than can read.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:31.520
And so, that's the positive end of the social
media explosion I would say.

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:33.520
The video and the podcast, in particular.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:34.820
Christina: Yes.

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:39.040
And what amazes me is when I hear...

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:45.830
I'll listen to tapes, you know, if you will,
lectures with Sam Harris or I don't know,

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:48.120
the other day, you with Dave Rubin...

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:49.660
Jordan: And Ben Shapiro.

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:51.720
Christina: ...and Ben Shapiro.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:58.210
And you do a little bit of politics, but then
it's onto minorities and you know, the Christian

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:01.410
theologians versus, you know, the Talmud actors.

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:03.380
It's very intellectual and...

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:06.410
Jordan: And that got a million views in some
already.

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:07.720
Christina: Even just instantly.

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:08.960
Jordan: Yeah, it's really something.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:11.410
And I found I couldn't stop listening.

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:19.210
And some of what you do reminds me of just
the delight in college courses before the

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:25.000
curriculum became race, class, gender, you
know, and the humanities at NYU in the, I

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.820
don't wanna say late '60s, it was the late
'60s.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:35.720
I took courses with Professor Jim Carson and
we would read Viktor Frankl and Mircea Eliade

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:40.220
and Paul Tillich, and Barth and the "Existentialism."

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:46.190
It was so stimulating and it was so thought-provoking
and wonderful.

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:48.479
And he was a great teacher.

00:06:48.479 --> 00:06:50.780
And it seems like that's not there anymore.

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:54.530
Like it was everybody that was interested
in ideas could have a semester.

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:58.940
I mean, now if you take philosophy, it's a
little arid and analytic, but there was the

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:03.610
philosophy of religion and there was the religion
department where you could have that.

00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:06.889
And his courses were so popular teaching these
writers.

00:07:06.889 --> 00:07:12.790
I mean, he could have enrolled the entire
school and I just thought like, "Okay, that's

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:13.790
what we're doing."

00:07:13.790 --> 00:07:17.030
Jordan: Well, you know, one of the advantages
to some degree, I've thought about this, is

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:22.820
that as the universities abandoned the classical
humanities, they leave that material around

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:24.110
for anyone else to pick up.

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:25.110
Christina: To us.

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:26.110
Jordan: Yeah.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:27.110
But that's it.

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:31.199
So, you know, there was a rule that I wrote,
"12 Rules for Life" was derived from an original

00:07:31.199 --> 00:07:38.740
list of 42 rules that I put on Quora and one
of them was, "Opportunity exists where responsibility

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:40.410
has been abdicated."

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:41.550
And I think that's really the case.

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:47.610
It's like maybe the postmodernists are right
and the classic humanities, the Western canon

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:48.860
has no value.

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:49.860
It's only arbitrary.

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:51.850
It's like, "So fine, they can abandon it.

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:53.430
Well, if it has no value, it'll disappear.

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:58.400
If it has value, then other people will find
the value and start to capitalize on it."

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:01.670
Let's say in terms of ability to attract audiences
and...

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:02.670
Christina: Yeah.

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:03.919
And the works of art will fight for themselves.

00:08:03.919 --> 00:08:09.260
Because as people read them, to read, you
know, Henry James or to read, you know, some

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:11.400
forgotten author that, you know, they're not
assigning.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.620
I've just been reading Saul Bellow.

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:15.120
He was just so good.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:16.120
And I...

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:17.120
Jordan: Yeah.

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:18.120
These people were actually good.

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:21.289
I mean, I have people all the time now who
come up to me and thank me because they've

00:08:21.289 --> 00:08:25.110
started to read great literature, but often
they've started with Dostoyevsky because I'm

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:31.010
such a Dostoyevsky fan and you know, they're
just completely knocked over well, by "Crime

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:33.870
and Punishment," which is a book that will
definitely knock you over.

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:35.291
Christina: I'm afraid to read it again.

00:08:35.291 --> 00:08:40.879
It knocked me over and is probably responsible
for me becoming so interested in philosophy

00:08:40.879 --> 00:08:42.969
in 11th grade.

00:08:42.969 --> 00:08:47.620
We had it with Ms. Sullivan and wow, it was
powerful.

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:54.569
Now I prefer, you know, Tolstoy and I told
my friend and she said, "Well, Dostoevsky's

00:08:54.569 --> 00:08:57.869
for teenagers and Tolstoy is very
when you're—" but... You don't agree?

00:08:57.869 --> 00:08:59.649
Jordan: Well, I don't know.

00:08:59.649 --> 00:09:05.170
I mean, I have read a couple of the books
of Dostoyevsky's books the second time and

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:10.619
I would say they didn't have the same impact
the second time, but I think that's also partly

00:09:10.619 --> 00:09:15.329
because they had such an impact the first
time, you know.

00:09:15.329 --> 00:09:20.230
And Tolstoy is more sociological in orientation
and, you know, maybe that is something that's

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:23.949
more interesting in some sense as you get
older.

00:09:23.949 --> 00:09:25.779
It's hard for me to say.

00:09:25.779 --> 00:09:29.980
I always preferred Dostoyevsky to Tolstoy
because I'm more psychologically minded.

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:30.980
But...

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:31.980
Christina: I love the idiot.

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:34.560
When I read that, I mean, not recently but
more recently.

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:39.569
And so I'm gonna give Dostoyevsky more of
a chance, but what you say is so important

00:09:39.569 --> 00:09:40.569
today.

00:09:40.569 --> 00:09:47.279
I think it was Andrea Gilles said that everything
important to say has been said but nobody's

00:09:47.279 --> 00:09:48.279
listening.

00:09:48.279 --> 00:09:50.490
So you have to say it again and again and
again.

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:53.279
And that's how I feel.

00:09:53.279 --> 00:10:00.009
I feel like I have to introduce college audiences
to the, you know, western political, you know,

00:10:00.009 --> 00:10:02.290
classical, liberal tradition.

00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:06.379
They may not have been acquainted with the
arguments for free speech and due process.

00:10:06.379 --> 00:10:09.369
Jordan: Very likely they're acquainted with
very little.

00:10:09.369 --> 00:10:13.790
Yeah, well, and that is a real opportunity
because those things are actually unbelievably

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:17.329
exciting if you can get into the spirit of
them, you know.

00:10:17.329 --> 00:10:21.389
And so that's something that I'm hoping to
do a lot more of in the upcoming years is

00:10:21.389 --> 00:10:26.769
to revitalize some of the fundamental classics
of the Western canon.

00:10:26.769 --> 00:10:32.629
I've also thought about the idea of the canon,
you know, because that's come under criticism.

00:10:32.629 --> 00:10:37.540
And I have a couple of associates who were
working with me on online education system

00:10:37.540 --> 00:10:42.660
and we've been trying to define what constitutes
the canon technically.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:44.220
And you can actually do that.

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:48.939
We don't have to argue about what the canon
is because imagine that you could rank order

00:10:48.939 --> 00:10:52.809
books in terms of the number of other books
that they've influenced?

00:10:52.809 --> 00:10:56.839
So obviously, the canon is made up of those
books that have had the most influence and

00:10:56.839 --> 00:10:58.559
so they're the most fundamental.

00:10:58.559 --> 00:11:03.100
Whether you agree with them or not is beside
the point, you need to know them because they're

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:05.819
at the bottom of your culture and that's you.

00:11:05.819 --> 00:11:11.100
And I mean, it's so funny because it's what
the social constructions claim, you're a product

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:12.100
of your culture.

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:15.499
It's like, well then you should probably know
it's fundamental writings since they make

00:11:15.499 --> 00:11:17.239
up
a huge part of your being.

00:11:17.239 --> 00:11:20.809
Christina: And you should know something about
a conversation that has been taking place

00:11:20.809 --> 00:11:27.130
among creative geniuses, over the centuries
with one another and how...

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:29.079
Jordan: Who else would you wanna listen to?

00:11:29.079 --> 00:11:30.649
Christina: Of course.

00:11:30.649 --> 00:11:34.989
And I always felt that I was just giving my
students this gift that they could sort of

00:11:34.989 --> 00:11:37.760
listen in and help them you know, approach
it.

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:42.240
And then suddenly, "Oh no, you know, we're
not teaching that anymore."

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:45.319
But I'm still hopeful that...

00:11:45.319 --> 00:11:50.410
Jordan: Well, I think the statement you made,
you know, that great art will speak for itself,

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:51.420
will fight for itself.

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:52.420
Christina: Will fight for itself.

00:11:52.420 --> 00:11:54.970
Jordan: Yeah, I think that's part of what
makes it great.

00:11:54.970 --> 00:11:58.350
Christina: We keep having secret meetings
with the intellectual dark web but we haven't

00:11:58.350 --> 00:11:59.899
had any, it's not a very good conspiracy.

00:11:59.899 --> 00:12:03.329
Jordan: No, no, it's like herding cats fundamentally.

00:12:03.329 --> 00:12:04.329
Christina: Yeah.

00:12:04.329 --> 00:12:09.200
But I think the success of it is owed not
because we feel like we're oppressed and we

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:15.119
have to speak in the dark but because I think
we give voice to ideas that many people have

00:12:15.119 --> 00:12:17.860
thought themselves, but maybe you didn't feel
confident to say.

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:21.269
Jordan: I think there's a difference too,
between being oppressed and being attacked

00:12:21.269 --> 00:12:22.269
by a minority.

00:12:22.269 --> 00:12:24.929
Like, you know, I certainly don't feel oppressed.

00:12:24.929 --> 00:12:29.209
I have more media reach than I know what to
do with.

00:12:29.209 --> 00:12:32.589
And most of what I've experienced from people
has been overwhelming support.

00:12:32.589 --> 00:12:34.410
There's a minority of noisy journalists, some...

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:37.130
Christina: Yeah, some snarky journalists and
some [crosstalk 00:12:37]

00:12:37.130 --> 00:12:41.129
Jordan: ...you know, kids on university campuses
that are deluded by their idiot professors.

00:12:41.129 --> 00:12:43.410
But other than that, it's been...

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:47.049
And I shouldn't minimize that because I was
under quite a lot of stress for the first

00:12:47.049 --> 00:12:50.420
year and a half or so that this all took place.

00:12:50.420 --> 00:12:54.920
But most of the consequences of that has been
extraordinarily positive.

00:12:54.920 --> 00:12:56.910
And I get to come here and talk to you, so
that's a good thing.

00:12:56.910 --> 00:13:00.670
Christina: Oh, it' so nice, finally, to be
able to sit down and talk with you.

00:13:00.670 --> 00:13:02.589
So thank you so much for coming to AEI.

00:13:02.589 --> 00:13:07.259
I hope you come back and I'll meet you out
there at the IDW.

00:13:07.259 --> 00:13:08.259
Jordan: Great, great.

00:13:08.259 --> 00:13:09.389
Very nice to see you.

00:13:09.389 --> 00:13:10.389
Yeah.

00:13:10.389 --> 00:13:11.809
Christina: Hey everyone.

00:13:11.809 --> 00:13:14.740
That's the end of our discussion with Jordan
Peterson.

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:15.740
Thanks for watching.

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:21.569
And if you've enjoyed what you saw, remember
to like the video or leave us a comment and

00:13:21.569 --> 00:13:24.230
be sure to check out the rest of our videos
and the research from AEI.

